Theory Array
An array is a collection of elements of the same data type stored in contiguous memory locations. Each element in an array is accessed using an index, where indexing starts from 0.

Array Declaration
Syntax:
data_type array_name[size];
Example :
int marks[5];

Initialize the Array-
Initialization means assigning initial values to array elements. We can initialize the array with values enclosed in curly braces '{}' are assigned to the array.
Example:
int marks[5] = {62, 94, 88, 72, 76};

Types of Arrays
1.One-dimensional Array :
A 1-D array is a single row of elements stored in a sequence under one name.
Each element can be accessed using an index starting from 0.
2.Two-dimensional (2D) array:
A 2-D array is like a table with rows and columns.
Each element is accessed using two indices: one for the row and one for the column.
It is used to store multiple values of the same type in a matrix-like structure.
Declaration of 2D Array
A 2D array with m rows and n columns can be created as:
Syntax- type arr_name[m][n];
3.Multidimensional Array (3D)
A Three-Dimensional Array or 3D array is a collection of two-dimensional arrays. It can be visualized as multiple 2D arrays stacked on top of each other.
Declaration of 3D Array
We can declare a 3D array with x 2D arrays each having m rows and n columns using the syntax shown below:
Syntax- type arr_name[x][m][n];

