Searching in Data Structures (DS)
Definition

Searching is the process of finding the location (index or position) of a specific element (called the key) in a data structure such as an array or linked list.

For example, in the array {10, 20, 30, 40, 50}, searching for 30 returns index 2.

Types of Searching

There are two main searching techniques:

1.Linear Search

2.Binary Search

1.Linear Search
Definition:
Linear Search checks each element one by one until the required element is found or the array ends.
Algorithm
1.Start from the first element.
2.Compare each element with the key.
3.If the key matches, return its position.
4.If the end of the array is reached without a match, the element is not found.

Example
Array: {12, 25, 30, 45, 50}

Search Key = 45
12 → 25 → 30 → 45 ✓ Found Pseudocode for Linear search-
#include <iostream> using namespace std;

int main()
{
int arr[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
int key, i;

cout << "Enter the element to search: "; cin >> key;

for (i = 0; i < 5; i++)
{
if (arr[i] == key)
{
cout << "Element found at position " << i + 1; return 0;
}
}
cout << "Element not found."; return 0;
}
Time Complexity

Best Case: O(1)
Average Case: O(n)
Worst Case: O(n)

2.Binary Search

Definition:
Binary Search searches an element by repeatedly dividing the sorted array into two halves.

Note: Binary Search works only on sorted arrays.

Algorithm
1.Find the middle element.
2.If the key equals the middle element, the search is successful.
3.If the key is smaller, search the left half.
4.If the key is larger, search the right half.
5.Repeat until the element is found or the search space becomes empty.

Example
Sorted Array: {10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70}
Search Key = 50
Step 1: Middle = 40
50 > 40 → Search Right
